## Context

The 2026-09-15T05:22Z sweep of the author's full library (sitter 0.4.15,
Zotero 10.0.2, pdf processor 14) stopped advancing on one document and was
ended by hand. The document is the IPCC AR6 WG1 report: **3 949 pages,
242 MB**, born digital, `itemID` 11940 / key `AI6DJUTA`.

What the trace shows, and only what it shows:

- progress ran **49 → 90** in 126 s, one tick every 2–3 s;
- then **90 held for at least 173 s**, phase still `extracting`, across three
  60 s heartbeats. The extension is alive throughout — the heartbeats prove it;
- two `switch enabled:false` records 11 s apart, then `shutdown reason:uninstall`.
- `storage/AI6DJUTA/` holds a `.zotero-ft-cache` and **no `.zotero-sdt-cache`**:
  no pack was persisted.

Nothing crashed. No `.dmp`, no `crashes/events` entry, no kernel OOM — and that
last zero is a read, not a refusal: `journalctl -k` returns 101 lines over the
same window.

The sitter does not extract. It calls `Zotero.SDT.ensure(id, {isPriority:false,
onProgress})` (`plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js:3412`) and the number it reports
is the native worker's own. `scheduler.js:305-312` documents that once handed to
the native worker the call cannot be told to stop — which is why the switch had
to be thrown twice.

**The open question, and the whole of this ticket:** at the observed rate the
remaining ~10 % (≈ 395 pages) should have taken ~25 s, not ≥ 173 s. Is the
native worker still advancing — coarse progress granularity, or a genuinely slow
tail — or is it wedged? Both answers are actionable and they point at different
fixes. Neither is established, and `scheduler.js:322-325` already warns that
from the sitter's side "a worker that ran out of memory and a photograph that
holds no text are indistinguishable".

Guard 2 of 0727 is unbuilt for exactly this reason: a check that cannot fire
must not ship. Same rule here — no sitter change lands off this trace alone.

## Relevant files

- `verification/incidents/0793-2026-09-15-ar6-stall.json` — the preserved trace,
  library shape, document metadata and negative checks. Item keys redacted to
  counts; the source file on the author's machine is overwritten at each shutdown.
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js:3412` — the `Zotero.SDT.ensure()` call site.
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js:283-330` — admit, progress tick, heartbeat,
  and the comments on uninterruptibility and on indistinguishable failures.
- `bench/sitter_acceptance.py` — the existing acceptance driver; the run harness
  to extend rather than improvise.
- `verification/acceptance/0.4.15-2026-09-14.json` — the record shape to follow.
- `~/data/Zotero-fresh/storage/AI6DJUTA/` — the document. Not in the repo, not
  redistributable (IPCC permits excerpts, not whole reports).

## Actions

1. Re-run the pass on `~/data/Zotero-fresh` with the AR6 admitted, and **let it
   sit for at least 30 minutes past the plateau** instead of switching off.
2. Sample, for the whole run: Zotero process CPU (per thread, so the document
   worker is separable), RSS, and read I/O. This is the discriminator — steady
   CPU on the worker thread means slow; ~0 % means wedged or waiting.
3. Record whether progress ever ticks past 90 and whether
   `storage/AI6DJUTA/.zotero-sdt-cache` appears.
4. Run the positive control of step "Test" below in the same session, so the
   probe is known to be able to see a completion.
5. Write the run up in `verification/incidents/` beside the preserved trace,
   naming which of slow / wedged holds and on what evidence.
6. **Settle what the progress number counts.** `scheduler.js` only relays it, so
   whether it tracks pages, bytes or something else is unread. It changes what a
   plateau means: if progress counts pages while the work per page varies, a bar
   advancing evenly says nothing about work remaining, and a stall at 90 on a
   document with heavy tail pages is expected rather than pathological. The bench
   is the plates volume filed in ticket 0794 — 758 pages whose individual
   character counts are now known, two modes a factor of 200 apart. Run it
   through `ensure()` and compare the progress ticks against the known per-page
   weights: ticks even across the plate run means pages, ticks clustered on the
   explication pages means text.

## Test

The red step is the **positive control**, not the AR6: a null on the AR6 means
nothing until the probe has been watched reacting to a document that completes.

Run the same instrumentation on `johnson-1785-dictionary` (1 104 pages, 124 MB,
already in the fixture cache) — it crosses the page cap, it finishes, and it is
the nearest thing in the corpus to the AR6. Record its progress granularity and
its tail timing: how long does *it* sit on its last tick before settling? If a
document that completes also plateaus for tens of seconds near the end, the AR6
plateau is a tail, not a wedge, and the whole reading changes.

## Verification

- [ ] CPU/RSS/IO series captured for the full AR6 attempt, worker thread separable
- [ ] Plateau observed for ≥ 30 min, or broken by a tick — with the timestamp either way
- [ ] `.zotero-sdt-cache` presence in `storage/AI6DJUTA/` recorded at the end
- [ ] Positive control (Johnson 1785) run in the same session, its tail timing recorded
- [ ] Verdict written as "slow" or "wedged" with the evidence that distinguishes them

## Invariants

- The author's live library is read, never rewritten; the pass runs on
  `~/data/Zotero-fresh`.
- No change to `plugins/sdt-sitter/` in this ticket. It measures; it does not fix.
- `make check` passes.

## Exit criteria

- One recorded run in `verification/incidents/` naming slow or wedged, with the
  positive control's numbers beside it.
- If **wedged**: an upstream report drafted, third alongside
  [133758](https://forums.zotero.org/discussion/133758/) and
  [133759](https://forums.zotero.org/discussion/133759/) — the sitter can neither
  cancel nor shut down while the native call hangs, which is the reportable part.
- If **slow**: the tail duration measured, and a recorded decision on whether the
  sitter should bound or announce it. That decision feeds 0792's bounded retry.
